// =============================================================================
// MemQL Spec Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `spec` syntax. It is NOT loaded by
// the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to
// skip it). Every spec-level annotation, every body classification, and
// every rejected form is demonstrated below with inline commentary.
//
// A spec is an atomic boolean predicate that binds to a concept (directly
// via @useConcept) or to a shape (transitively via @useShape). The body is
// a single boolean expression. The expression's field references
// implicitly classify the spec as a row-spec (compiles to a SQL WHERE
// fragment) or a context-spec (evaluates in-process against the auth
// envelope).
//
// Spec vs Trait -- the one-line summary:
//
//                       Trait                       Spec
//   binding              forbidden                  REQUIRED (one of two)
//   @useConcept          rejected                   accepted
//   @useShape            rejected                   accepted
//   body classification  row-spec OR context-spec   row-spec OR context-spec
//   author lifecycle     @enabled / @disabled OK    NOT ALLOWED
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. Spec-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
// Every annotation goes above the `spec` keyword. The full author surface
// is three annotations -- and exactly one of @useConcept / @useShape is
// REQUIRED.
//
//   @description("text")           Human-readable description.           optional
//   @useConcept(<bareName>)        Concept binding. Spec body references
//                                  payload fields as `<bareName>.X`.    REQUIRED*
//   @useShape(<bareName>)          Shape binding. The shape's concept
//                                  becomes the spec's effective concept;
//                                  body references payload fields as
//                                  `<bareName>.X`.                       REQUIRED*
//
//   (* exactly one of @useConcept or @useShape -- neither = error,
//      both = error)
//
// One internal sentinel is injected by the rewriter -- never write it by
// hand:
//
//   @_isTrait                      Synthetic marker injected when the
//                                  declaration is `trait` rather than
//                                  `spec`. Specs never carry it.
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. EXPLICITLY REJECTED on specs
// =============================================================================
//
//   @enabled  /  @disabled
//     The engine owns spec lifecycle, not authors. Specs either exist (in
//     which case they're live) or they don't. Use git to add / remove a
//     spec; do not toggle it via annotation. Error messages:
//
//        `spec %q must not carry @enabled (the engine controls spec
//         lifecycle; remove the annotation)`
//        `spec %q must not carry @disabled (the engine controls spec
//         lifecycle; delete the spec instead)`
//
//   @shape("name")
//     The legacy single-string binding form. Retired. Use the canonical
//     @useShape(<bareName>) form instead.
//
// =============================================================================
//   3. Concept-bound spec: the most common case
// =============================================================================
//
// A spec bound via @useConcept(<name>) names a concept directly. Inside
// the body, payload fields are written as `<name>.X` for readability and
// rewritten to `payload.X` by the loader before parse.

@useConcept(participant)
@description("Matches participants with human participantType")
spec specIsHumanParticipant {
  participant.participantType == "human"
}

@useConcept(participant)
@description("Matches participants with SI participantType")
spec specIsSIParticipant {
  participant.participantType == "si"
}

// =============================================================================
//   4. Shape-bound spec: opt into a row shape's projection
// =============================================================================
//
// A spec bound via @useShape(<shapeName>) binds to a shape; the shape's
// concept becomes the spec's effective concept. Same body-reference rule:
// payload fields written as `<shapeName>.X` are rewritten to `payload.X`.
//
// Use @useShape when the spec is naturally tied to a SHAPE'S projection
// rather than to the concept's full payload -- e.g. a spec that only
// references fields the shape projects.

@useShape(participantFull)
@description("Same predicate as specIsHumanParticipant, bound via the shape that owns the projection.")
spec specIsHumanParticipantViaShape {
  participantFull.participantType == "human"
}

// =============================================================================
//   5. Trait-shape-bound spec: caller-only or cross-concept predicates
// =============================================================================
//
// A trait shape is a @row shape without @concepts(...) -- a concept-
// agnostic scaffold. Binding a spec to one expresses "this predicate
// applies to any row that matches the trait shape's projection" without
// committing to a single concept.
//
// Trait shapes the tree ships today: callerActor (caller envelope),
// activeRowTrait, statusRowTrait, deletedRowTrait, archivedRowTrait,
// savedRowTrait, validationRowTrait, etc.

@useShape(callerActor)
@description("Caller must hold an admin or owner role.")
spec requiresAdmin {
  caller.role == "admin" || caller.role == "owner"
}

@useShape(statusRowTrait)
@description("Any row whose status is 'active' regardless of concept.")
spec specStatusActive {
  payload.status == "active"
}

// =============================================================================
//   6. Body classification: row-spec vs context-spec
// =============================================================================
//
// Implicit. The body's field references decide:
//
//   payload.* / id / concept / type / createdAt / createdBy / schema / meta
//     -> ROW-SPEC. Compiles to a SQL WHERE fragment. Pushed down to the
//        database. Composable in query filter clauses via:
//
//            filter <expr>; specName
//
//   caller.*
//     -> CONTEXT-SPEC. Evaluates in-process against the auth envelope.
//        Callable from policy bodies via `spec("name")`. Not pushed down
//        to SQL -- engine.EvaluateSpec(ctx, name) returns the boolean.
//
// Mixed bodies (both row + caller refs) are REJECTED at load time:
//
//   `spec body mixes row references (payload / intrinsics) with context
//    references (caller.*) -- split into a row-spec + a context-spec and
//    compose via a policy`
//
// Split mixed predicates into two specs + compose via a policy that
// invokes spec("row") AND spec("context").

@useConcept(participant)
@description("Row-spec: payload-only body. Compiles to SQL.")
spec rowSpecExample {
  payload.status == "active"
}

@useShape(callerActor)
@description("Context-spec: caller-only body. Evaluates in-process.")
spec contextSpecExample {
  caller.userId != null
}

// =============================================================================
//   7. Caller envelope vocabulary
// =============================================================================
//
// Context-spec bodies can reference these caller.* fields:
//
//   caller.userId          authenticated user's id (string)
//   caller.role            cluster-wide role (owner / admin / writer / reader)
//   caller.identityId      credential's identity id
//   caller.isClusterOwner  bool, true for the cluster owner
//   caller.partitions      []string of partitions the caller may touch
//   caller.partition       current request's partition
//   caller.now             RFC3339 timestamp captured at request start
//   caller.config.<key>    allow-listed config values (see policy_exposable.go)

@useShape(callerActor)
@description("Caller is the cluster owner.")
spec specIsClusterOwner {
  caller.isClusterOwner == true
}

// =============================================================================
//   8. Row intrinsics: bare-name references inside row-specs
// =============================================================================
//
// Row-spec bodies can reference these engine-stamped intrinsics by their
// bare name (no `payload.` prefix):
//
//   id          row id
//   createdAt   insert timestamp
//   createdBy   author identity id
//   concept     canonical concept id
//   type        the concept's @type value
//   schema      schema variant id
//   partition   partition the row lives in
//
// The same spec can mix payload fields AND intrinsics.

@useConcept(participant)
@description("Row authored by a specific seed identity.")
spec specIsSeedParticipant {
  createdBy == "seed" && payload.participantType == "human"
}

// =============================================================================
//   9. Spec body operator vocabulary
// =============================================================================
//
// Comparison operators: == != > >= < <=
// Logical operators:    && || !
// Set operators:        in (X, Y, Z)
// Null check:           field == null  /  field != null
// Parentheses:          (expr1) || (expr2 && expr3)

@useConcept(participant)
@description("Rich predicate showing the operator surface.")
spec specRichPredicate {
  payload.participantType == "human"
  && (payload.status == "active" || payload.status == "invited")
  && payload.tier != "banned"
  && payload.score >= 50
}

// =============================================================================
//   10. Calling specs
// =============================================================================
//
// Specs register under their declared name. Callers reference them like
// any other named predicate:
//
//   - From a query's filter clause: list the spec name alongside other
//     predicates, separated by `;`:
//
//         use cognition.participant
//
//         query queryActiveHumanParticipants {
//           args { spaceId string @required }
//           filter participant.spaceId==args.spaceId; specIsHumanParticipant
//           shape  participantFull
//         }
//
//   - From a policy body: use `spec("name")`. Both row-specs and
//     context-specs can be composed this way, though only context-specs
//     evaluate without a row context.
//
//         func (Policy) canViewAdmin(_ any) bool {
//           ctx.output = spec("requiresAdmin")
//           return ctx, nil
//         }
//
//   - From Go: engine.EvaluateSpec(ctx, "specName") evaluates a
//     context-spec against the request's auth context. Row-specs cannot
//     be called this way -- they require a row to evaluate.
//
// =============================================================================
//   11. Forbidden binding combinations
// =============================================================================
//
// All three of these are rejected at load time:
//
//   // REJECTED: missing binding.
//   @description("no binding")
//   spec specNoBinding {
//     payload.active == true
//   }
//
//   // REJECTED: cannot carry both bindings.
//   @useConcept(participant)
//   @useShape(participantFull)
//   @description("both bindings")
//   spec specBothBindings {
//     participant.active == true
//   }
//
//   // REJECTED: legacy single-string @shape form retired.
//   @shape("participantFull")
//   @description("legacy form")
//   spec specLegacyShape {
//     payload.active == true
//   }
//
// =============================================================================
//   12. The legacy `func (Spec) name(...) bool { ... }` form -- retired
// =============================================================================
//
// The struct-form `spec NAME { <expr> }` is the only spec declaration the
// parser accepts. The procedural function-receiver form was retired
// during the policy-first migration. The current parser will reject any
// attempt at the legacy form.
//
//     // NOT VALID -- struct form is required.
//     func (Spec) isActive(_ any) bool { return payload.active == true }
